  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` couldn't handle prices with a thousands separator, such as `\"1,005.99\"`. Its regex didn't match, so `m` was `null` and `m[1]` threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. `POST /checkout` turned that into a 500.\n\nThe log supports this. Every failing checkout payload I extracted had a price like `9,999.99`: 37 requests, all with a comma. The 1,152 payloads without a comma (`9`, `9.99`, `99.99`, `999.99`) all succeeded. That's why the errors looked intermittent: they only hit orders containing an item priced at 1,000 or more.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- The regex now accepts properly grouped thousands (`1,005.99`, `1,234,567`).\n- Input that still doesn't match, like `abc` or `1,23.00`, now throws the documented `Invalid amount: <input>` error instead of a `TypeError`. A malformed price will still produce a 500 from the server. I didn't change that handling.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):** I added three regression tests, covering comma-grouped amounts, the invalid-amount error, and a checkout total with a `1,005.99` price. `npm test` passes with 7 of 7 tests. I didn't run the new tests against the old code to confirm they fail there. The comma cases would hit the same `TypeError` that `ord_14143` hit in the log.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
